 ## 3. Known weaknesses and next steps
 - The Joseph form is not a square-root filter. On very ill-conditioned updates the step guard turns rounding failure into `Err(SingularMatrix)` instead of producing a usable P. A UD or square-root update would let those steps succeed.
 - The builder and helper can't guard against huge but *non-overflowing* sizes on overcommitting systems. `try_reserve_exact` may succeed and touching the memory may then OOM. This only affects `Diagonal`/`Scalar` R with m ≫ n, or `try_constant_velocity` with a huge `axes`.
+- The smoother needs the one-step predicted covariance `P⁻_{k+1}` to be numerically positive definite, because its gain is a Cholesky solve with it; the filter itself only needs `S = H P⁻ Hᵀ + R`. On the two SPEC B11 models in f32 it therefore reports the failure instead of returning an uncertified covariance: model A (`P0 = 1e8·I`, `R = 1e-8`) gives `SingularMatrix` because the exact posterior after the first update is rank one and `Q = 1e-6` is below f32 resolution at 5e7, and model B (nearly collinear H) gives `FilterDivergence` from the same PSD guard `commit` uses, because the backward sum loses about 5e-2 of the largest eigenvalue. Both models pass in f64. A square-root or two-filter smoother would avoid the failure.
 - §7 out-of-scope items:
